WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.583
JENNY BROWN: Uploading
and downloading

00:00:01.583 --> 00:00:03.210
is all fine and good,
but sometimes you

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.940
need to do more maintenance
on the data in your buckets.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.500
Today on Cloud
Storage Bytes, we're

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.150
going to dig into
Object Management.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.595
So stick around.

00:00:10.595 --> 00:00:13.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.810
No matter how you get your
data into Google Cloud Storage,

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.780
once it's there, it's
manageable, meaning easy

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:21.800
to manage.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.420
Just how easy?

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.270
Easy as one, two, three.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.070
Let's talk about lists.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.268
You see what I did there?

00:00:29.268 --> 00:00:31.310
Anytime you want to do
something with the objects

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:33.650
in your bucket, it helps
to know what's there.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.050
Starting with a list will get
you the inventory you need

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:40.190
of all of those 11,723
data objects you've got.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:42.740
But if you've somehow
got that memorized,

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.360
feel free to skip ahead.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.340
As we talked about
when getting data in,

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.500
there are two main
options for interacting

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.070
with your cloud storage, command
line gsutil and the Cloud

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:53.150
console.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.570
Spoiler alert, this is going
to be true for a while.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.060
So listing the contents
of bucket with gsutil,

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.170
you'll get a nice
structured response,

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:04.310
making it easy to see
what you've got and where.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.230
And of course, the
Cloud console offers

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.990
a convenient visual interface
with dropdowns and buttons

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.920
for looking at all of your
files and directories.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.670
So now that we know
what's in your bucket,

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.560
let's talk changes.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.530
Rename an object with
a few specific steps.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.200
Copy the object, making
another instance of it,

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.500
either in the current bucket
or in any other bucket.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.470
Move the object
into a new bucket.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.120
And for a more
permanent change, you

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.060
can always delete an object,
removing it from the bucket

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.240
and, effectively, from
your cloud storage.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:36.980
The command line
utility was designed

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:40.760
to be as easy to remember and
as easy to navigate as the Cloud

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:41.688
console.

00:01:41.688 --> 00:01:43.730
So these commands might
even look familiar to you

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.340
if you're used to working
in the command terminal.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.620
No matter how you work with
it, Google Cloud Storage

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.120
is going to give
you the flexibility

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.305
you need to create the perfect
solution for your needs.

00:01:53.305 --> 00:01:54.680
Now, there's plenty
more that you

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.780
can do with your data
and Google Cloud Storage,

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.580
but that's for another episode.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.400
Subscribe, give
us a like, and let

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.150
us know what features
of Cloud Storage you

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:03.650
want to learn more about.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.140
Thanks for joining us for this
quick byte of Google Cloud

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:06.860
Storage.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.000
See you next time.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

